==English==
[[File:Bunks in a Seven-Cent Lodging-House, Pell Street.jpeg|thumb|upright|Bunks in a '''flophouse''', circa 1890.]]

===Alternative forms===
* [[flop house]]

===Etymology===
US 1904, {{compound|flop|house}}, originally [[hobo]] slang, presumably from slang {{term|flop||lie down to sleep|lang=en}}.<ref name="oetyld">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> Other speculative etymologies relate it to flopping the men out of hanging bunks by letting down the ropes. The type of cheap hotel dates to the 19th century, and was originally called a {{term|lodging house|lang=en}} or {{term|workingmen's hotel|workingmen’s hotel|lang=en}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cheap]] [[hotel]] or [[boarding house]] where many people sleep in large rooms.
#* '''1904''', ''[[w:McClure's|McClure’s Magazine]],'' November 1904:<ref name="oetyld" />
#*: In one of [Cincinnati’s] slum districts stands the Silver Moon, a “'''flop house'''” (i.e., a house where the occupants are “flopped” out of their hanging bunks by letting down the ropes).

